## General User Workflow

The `ordinal` package provides a cumulative link modeling workflow in which
a user normally starts from a proportional-odds model and only introduces
partial proportional-odds effects when the simpler model is not adequate.

For the logit link, the general workflow is:

```text
clm()
  ↓
summary() / convergence()
  ↓
nominal_test() + scale_test()
  ↓
Researcher decision
  ↓
clm(..., nominal = ...)
  ↓
anova()
  ↓
summary() / convergence()
  ↓
predict()

in detail:

1. `clm()`

Fit a cumulative link model.

Syntax
```r
clm(
    formula,
    scale,
    nominal,
    data,
    weights,
    start,
    subset,
    doFit = TRUE,
    na.action,
    contrasts,
    model = TRUE,
    control = list(),
    link = c(
        "logit",
        "probit",
        "cloglog",
        "loglog",
        "cauchit",
        "Aranda-Ordaz",
        "log-gamma"
    ),
    threshold = c(
        "flexible",
        "symmetric",
        "symmetric2",
        "equidistant"
    ),
    ...
)
```
Arguments
`formula`
Input: Formula
```r
response ~ x1 + x2
```
Purpose:  
Defines the ordinal response and the ordinary location predictors.
PPO relevance:  
Predictors in this part have common effects across thresholds unless they are modeled through another component.
---
`scale`
Input: One-sided formula
```r
~ x
```
Purpose:  
Models differences in the scale / dispersion of the latent distribution.
PPO relevance:  
Scale heterogeneity can be an alternative explanation for apparent non-proportionality.
---
`nominal`
Input: One-sided formula
```r
~ x
```
Purpose:  
Allows predictor effects to vary across thresholds.
PPO relevance:  
With the logit link, nominal effects provide the main PPO mechanism in `ordinal`.
---
`data`
Input: Data frame
Contains the variables used by the model.
---
`weights`
Input: Numeric vector
Controls the contribution of each observation to the likelihood.
By default, each observation has weight 1.
---
`start`
Input: Numeric vector
Initial parameter values used by the optimizer.
The parameter vector may contain:
threshold parameters (`alpha`);
location parameters (`beta`);
scale parameters (`zeta`).
Normally this argument does not need to be specified by ordinary users.
---
`subset`
Input: Logical or indexing expression
Selects which observations are used for model fitting.
---
`doFit`
Input: Logical
Default:
```r
TRUE
```
Controls whether the model is actually fitted after its internal structure is constructed.
---
`na.action`
Purpose:  
Controls how missing values are handled.
---
`contrasts`
Purpose:  
Controls how categorical predictors are encoded into the model matrix.
This affects coefficient parameterization and can matter when comparing results across software.
---
`model`
Input: Logical
Default:
```r
TRUE
```
Controls whether the fitted model frame is stored in the model object.
---
`control`
Input: Control list
Controls numerical optimization and convergence behavior.
---
`link`
Input: Character string
Available links include:
```text
logit
probit
cloglog
loglog
cauchit
Aranda-Ordaz
log-gamma
```
Purpose:  
Defines the transformation connecting cumulative probabilities to the linear predictor.
PPO relevance:  
The term Partial Proportional Odds applies specifically to the logit-link case.
---
`threshold`
Input: Character string
Available threshold structures include:
```text
flexible
symmetric
symmetric2
equidistant
```
Purpose:  
Controls structural constraints on category thresholds / cut-points.
---
2. `summary()`
Purpose
Display the main results of a fitted `clm` model.
Input
A fitted `clm` object.
```r
summary(model)
```
Main Output
coefficient estimates;
standard errors;
z statistics;
p-values;
threshold estimates;
log-likelihood;
AIC;
iteration information;
maximum gradient;
Hessian condition information.
---
3. `nominal_test()`
Purpose
Assess whether predictors may require threshold-specific effects.
Syntax
```r
nominal_test(
    object,
    scope,
    trace = FALSE,
    ...
)
```
Arguments
`object`
A fitted `clm` model used as the baseline model.
`scope`
Specifies which predictors should be tested for nominal / threshold-specific effects.
Main Output
For each tested predictor:
degrees of freedom;
log-likelihood;
AIC;
likelihood-ratio statistic;
p-value.
PPO Relevance
Provides predictor-level evidence about whether the proportional-odds constraint may be too restrictive.
It does not automatically select the final PPO specification.
---
4. `scale_test()`
Purpose
Assess whether adding scale effects improves the fitted cumulative link model.
Input
A fitted `clm` object.
```r
scale_test(model)
```
Main Output
For each tested predictor:
degrees of freedom;
log-likelihood;
AIC;
likelihood-ratio statistic;
p-value.
PPO Relevance
Helps distinguish scale heterogeneity from genuine threshold-specific effects.
---
5. `anova()`
Purpose
Compare nested cumulative link models.
Typical Usage
```r
anova(m_po, m_ppo)
```
Main Output
number of parameters;
AIC;
log-likelihood;
likelihood-ratio statistic;
degrees of freedom;
p-value.
PPO Relevance
Can be used to assess whether relaxing proportional-odds constraints produces a sufficient improvement in model fit.
---
6. `convergence()`
Purpose
Assess the numerical accuracy of fitted maximum-likelihood estimates.
Input
A fitted `clm` object.
```r
convergence(model)
```
Main Information
parameter error;
correct decimals;
significant digits;
log-likelihood error.
Interpretation
`summary()` shows what the model estimated.
`convergence()` evaluates how numerically accurate those estimates are.
---
7. `predict()`
Purpose
Generate predictions from a fitted PO or PPO model.
Typical Syntax
```r
predict(
    object,
    newdata,
    se.fit = FALSE,
    interval = FALSE,
    level = 0.95,
    type = "prob"
)
```
Arguments
`object`
A fitted PO or PPO `clm` model.
`newdata`
New predictor values for which predictions are required.
`se.fit`
Requests standard errors for predictions.
`interval`
Requests confidence intervals.
`level`
Confidence level used for intervals.
Default:
```r
0.95
```
`type`
Main options:
```text
prob
class
cum.prob
linear.predictor
```
Main Output
Depending on `type`, the function can return:
category probabilities;
cumulative probabilities;
predicted categories;
linear predictor values.
PPO Relevance
Predicted probabilities are often easier to interpret than threshold-specific PPO coefficients.
